What would you do if both UAT and Staging applications went down at the same time?
This is one of those situations where it's easy to panic and immediately restart everything.
But as a DevOps Engineer, the first step isn't to fix. It's to understand why it happened.
Here's how I'd approach it:
🔍 First, I'd confirm that the application is actually down by checking monitoring dashboards, alerts, and the overall health of the environment.
Then I'd start narrowing down the possible causes.
• Are the servers, ECS services, or Kubernetes pods healthy?
• Is the Load Balancer routing traffic correctly?
• Has the database become unreachable?
• Are there any recent deployments that could have introduced the issue?
• Did someone update environment variables, Secrets, or ConfigMaps?
• Are application logs showing any errors?
• Is there a cloud service or network issue affecting both environments?
The interesting part is that both UAT and Staging are down together.
Whenever multiple environments fail at the same time, I stop thinking about the application first and start looking for what they have in common.
Maybe they're using:
✅ The same Kubernetes cluster
✅ The same database
✅ The same Load Balancer
✅ The same CI/CD pipeline
✅ The same shared infrastructure
Finding that common dependency usually helps identify the root cause much faster.
